[{:db/id 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cd56ae3f-1187-5181-af79-1866b5188a10" "question-00083281-29d7-555a-802b-bb136fd26b95"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5f2b40e8-b423-5c7a-b959-43a67763f792" "question-28a83ce5-6b28-57bd-96e8-b831ae6041a8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2ce23f40-f256-54cd-9b13-888ae9d3dff9" "question-09c354f4-ffe0-5d80-b23d-97b7671b8b06"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cd56ae3f-1187-5181-af79-1866b5188a10" :question/id #uuid "cd56ae3f-1187-5181-af79-1866b5188a10" :question/math-academy-id "q-162575" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+xyz\\,j+2y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y) \\\\  & =2x+xz+0 \\\\  & =2x+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+xz)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,2x+xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89b39073-1256-589c-ae05-81365bf7916b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89b39073-1256-589c-ae05-81365bf7916b" :answer-field/id #uuid "89b39073-1256-589c-ae05-81365bf7916b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-06c35222-68d1-50e1-b3be-f3859d1566f3" "answer-069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" "answer-165ccd61-a96d-55ca-a818-a06291f0c969" "answer-fd490bcb-15ef-5833-9df8-21e37b4504ad" "answer-661de3a9-92bc-5da3-91fa-17df7bbafc4f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06c35222-68d1-50e1-b3be-f3859d1566f3" :answer/id #uuid "06c35222-68d1-50e1-b3be-f3859d1566f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" :answer/id #uuid "069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+xz" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-165ccd61-a96d-55ca-a818-a06291f0c969" :answer/id #uuid "165ccd61-a96d-55ca-a818-a06291f0c969" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+2z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd490bcb-15ef-5833-9df8-21e37b4504ad" :answer/id #uuid "fd490bcb-15ef-5833-9df8-21e37b4504ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+yz" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-661de3a9-92bc-5da3-91fa-17df7bbafc4f" :answer/id #uuid "661de3a9-92bc-5da3-91fa-17df7bbafc4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-00083281-29d7-555a-802b-bb136fd26b95" :question/id #uuid "00083281-29d7-555a-802b-bb136fd26b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-162586" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x-z+2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=z\\,i-4y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0-4+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x-z+2\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ef02b27-25c8-5d51-9b89-2000755f118a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ef02b27-25c8-5d51-9b89-2000755f118a" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef02b27-25c8-5d51-9b89-2000755f118a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-499cdd5b-e6c0-5bd4-bfc7-e7acaa3926fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e983312-df08-546e-9806-4e462f4e1659" "answer-688adcb4-8f82-5557-b006-90cbca149a33" "answer-499cdd5b-e6c0-5bd4-bfc7-e7acaa3926fb" "answer-8128a8cf-b3f9-556e-aefb-154a9c6699fb" "answer-d03cded5-b5e9-5895-a862-9145e719e5f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e983312-df08-546e-9806-4e462f4e1659" :answer/id #uuid "4e983312-df08-546e-9806-4e462f4e1659" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-688adcb4-8f82-5557-b006-90cbca149a33" :answer/id #uuid "688adcb4-8f82-5557-b006-90cbca149a33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2}\\,{z}^{3}-3\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-499cdd5b-e6c0-5bd4-bfc7-e7acaa3926fb" :answer/id #uuid "499cdd5b-e6c0-5bd4-bfc7-e7acaa3926fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8128a8cf-b3f9-556e-aefb-154a9c6699fb" :answer/id #uuid "8128a8cf-b3f9-556e-aefb-154a9c6699fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d03cded5-b5e9-5895-a862-9145e719e5f1" :answer/id #uuid "d03cded5-b5e9-5895-a862-9145e719e5f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,{z}^{3}-3\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5f2b40e8-b423-5c7a-b959-43a67763f792" :question/id #uuid "5f2b40e8-b423-5c7a-b959-43a67763f792" :question/math-academy-id "q-162574" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the vector field $F=(yz-2x)\\,i+3y\\,j+(x+z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+z) \\\\  & =-2+3+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $3$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{3} \\\\  & =72\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b15f2475-f304-5fb8-a434-536b4a248735"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b15f2475-f304-5fb8-a434-536b4a248735" :answer-field/id #uuid "b15f2475-f304-5fb8-a434-536b4a248735" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-41e506e3-ebbe-5f8b-ae29-c18276419687" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6aacb83d-c030-5c62-a7af-140b5f553083" "answer-ceca5263-40f9-5ba9-8496-31c6fead0bd7" "answer-5b7901d4-d410-5e21-8e33-71572db7a730" "answer-41e506e3-ebbe-5f8b-ae29-c18276419687" "answer-3b92e2ac-43e0-56d7-9ecd-75553160a01c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6aacb83d-c030-5c62-a7af-140b5f553083" :answer/id #uuid "6aacb83d-c030-5c62-a7af-140b5f553083" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ceca5263-40f9-5ba9-8496-31c6fead0bd7" :answer/id #uuid "ceca5263-40f9-5ba9-8496-31c6fead0bd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b7901d4-d410-5e21-8e33-71572db7a730" :answer/id #uuid "5b7901d4-d410-5e21-8e33-71572db7a730" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41e506e3-ebbe-5f8b-ae29-c18276419687" :answer/id #uuid "41e506e3-ebbe-5f8b-ae29-c18276419687" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b92e2ac-43e0-56d7-9ecd-75553160a01c" :answer/id #uuid "3b92e2ac-43e0-56d7-9ecd-75553160a01c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-28a83ce5-6b28-57bd-96e8-b831ae6041a8" :question/id #uuid "28a83ce5-6b28-57bd-96e8-b831ae6041a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162573" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dc4f6d9e8520dead79789efd4f08c07ea7921d28f4cf6f8da06de533d012e90.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y-z,\\,y-2x,\\,x+4z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+4z) \\\\  & =0+1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}5\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =5{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d4a0d219-93b4-5cee-98eb-dc3219549951"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4a0d219-93b4-5cee-98eb-dc3219549951" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a0d219-93b4-5cee-98eb-dc3219549951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6b730726-fed2-5b3e-8ffc-354b2c3bf2f7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3e6b3f5a-f220-5554-9220-850158e49738" "answer-19f3436d-bc76-5261-8ad1-368ca3b77f95" "answer-e4005834-c2ac-5578-8587-1d569f479bce" "answer-6b730726-fed2-5b3e-8ffc-354b2c3bf2f7" "answer-e993ee67-8cbb-5ee4-a9bb-9dc04ab4bbe5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e6b3f5a-f220-5554-9220-850158e49738" :answer/id #uuid "3e6b3f5a-f220-5554-9220-850158e49738" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19f3436d-bc76-5261-8ad1-368ca3b77f95" :answer/id #uuid "19f3436d-bc76-5261-8ad1-368ca3b77f95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4005834-c2ac-5578-8587-1d569f479bce" :answer/id #uuid "e4005834-c2ac-5578-8587-1d569f479bce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b730726-fed2-5b3e-8ffc-354b2c3bf2f7" :answer/id #uuid "6b730726-fed2-5b3e-8ffc-354b2c3bf2f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e993ee67-8cbb-5ee4-a9bb-9dc04ab4bbe5" :answer/id #uuid "e993ee67-8cbb-5ee4-a9bb-9dc04ab4bbe5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ce23f40-f256-54cd-9b13-888ae9d3dff9" :question/id #uuid "2ce23f40-f256-54cd-9b13-888ae9d3dff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162605" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(y+{z}^{2})\\,i-x\\,j+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot [{e}^{z}]_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\cdot 2\\cdot ({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f84ddbb-0ce0-5549-8bb9-1dfef0b987f0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f84ddbb-0ce0-5549-8bb9-1dfef0b987f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f84ddbb-0ce0-5549-8bb9-1dfef0b987f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa91438f-d562-5478-84cb-9cc0479a64c0" "answer-24ea0e2b-5e7a-52c9-a1b3-f61827c82bd8" "answer-727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" "answer-676e7034-5bd6-59ee-bc64-fafa0a12a224" "answer-dc5308bb-1224-54c9-a249-060024986a6d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa91438f-d562-5478-84cb-9cc0479a64c0" :answer/id #uuid "fa91438f-d562-5478-84cb-9cc0479a64c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24ea0e2b-5e7a-52c9-a1b3-f61827c82bd8" :answer/id #uuid "24ea0e2b-5e7a-52c9-a1b3-f61827c82bd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer/id #uuid "727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-676e7034-5bd6-59ee-bc64-fafa0a12a224" :answer/id #uuid "676e7034-5bd6-59ee-bc64-fafa0a12a224" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc5308bb-1224-54c9-a249-060024986a6d" :answer/id #uuid "dc5308bb-1224-54c9-a249-060024986a6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-09c354f4-ffe0-5d80-b23d-97b7671b8b06" :question/id #uuid "09c354f4-ffe0-5d80-b23d-97b7671b8b06" :question/math-academy-id "q-162595" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y{z}^{2}\\,i-2yz\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2z)+6z \\\\  & =4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [2{z}^{2}]_{z=-1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (8-2) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc27d6bf-860e-505c-82f5-df4b985c7b2d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc27d6bf-860e-505c-82f5-df4b985c7b2d" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc27d6bf-860e-505c-82f5-df4b985c7b2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-475b50f4-a66e-582c-bb09-b18513917dc7" "answer-ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" "answer-23e226ce-01b7-5120-bfc5-5949d300bfcf" "answer-12f5be7e-0bfc-59a7-af3a-6dfcf5d7d844" "answer-e03f06d7-09f6-5991-8022-a31e044488bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-475b50f4-a66e-582c-bb09-b18513917dc7" :answer/id #uuid "475b50f4-a66e-582c-bb09-b18513917dc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer/id #uuid "ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23e226ce-01b7-5120-bfc5-5949d300bfcf" :answer/id #uuid "23e226ce-01b7-5120-bfc5-5949d300bfcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12f5be7e-0bfc-59a7-af3a-6dfcf5d7d844" :answer/id #uuid "12f5be7e-0bfc-59a7-af3a-6dfcf5d7d844" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e03f06d7-09f6-5991-8022-a31e044488bf" :answer/id #uuid "e03f06d7-09f6-5991-8022-a31e044488bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135160 :tutorial/content "Consider a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ defined over a region $D.$ \n\nRecall that Green's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the boundary of $D.$ \n\nWe can express Green's theorem in flux form as a single picture, shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52191ef5fea6e08b885dfd9ffea547e3843b8113847b532f8b4aa5d966d15213.png)\n\n    \n\nWe'll now state the higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. Please note that we will restrict our attention to so-called **simple regions** for the remainder of this lesson. Simple regions in $\\mathbb R^3$ can be classified as type I, type II, *and* type III." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135170 :tutorial/content "The **divergence theorem** is a higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. It states the following:\n\n*Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then*\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}\\:\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\n*where $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal to $S.$*\n\nIn other words, the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $R$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through the boundary of $R.$ \n\nThe divergence theorem can be summarized in a single diagram.\n\n![](images/ff/ffabcceb5f4fd520a57f909c476b267f584ac4dc3fb948a5c1996b71dbc74e6f.png)\n\nThe divergence theorem can be used to simplify flux calculations. Calculating the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across a surface can be time-consuming because we often need to consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across multiple faces that make up $S.$ The divergence theorem often allows us to reduce a complicated flux calculation to a single triple integral." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135164 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 2x+1,\\: 3z,\\: xy \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown below. \n\n![](images/cf/cf90197ac99a066db75154200de4cbee2494fef15deed6be7b7c4cd501ae8f26.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nTo find the total flux directly, we would need to find the flux through each of the six surfaces of our rectangular solid and then add the results. However, using the divergence theorem\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwe can reduce this computation to a single triple integral.\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =2+0+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux through $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134012 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
